________________________________________
1. Relationship Between Kubernetes and OpenShift
Kubernetes
Kubernetes is a container orchestration platform that manages:
•	container deployment
•	scaling
•	networking
•	cluster management
But Kubernetes only provides basic building blocks.
Example:
Kubernetes provides:
•	Pods
•	Services
•	Deployments
•	Networking primitives
But it does not include full developer tools.
________________________________________
OpenShift
OpenShift is a complete container platform built on Kubernetes.
It adds many features to Kubernetes to make it enterprise-ready.
Simple idea:
Containers
     ↓
Kubernetes (manages containers)
     ↓
OpenShift (complete platform built on Kubernetes)
So:
OpenShift = Kubernetes + extra enterprise features
________________________________________
2. Major Features Added by OpenShift
OpenShift extends Kubernetes with additional services.
Main additions:
1.	Developer workflow
2.	Observability
3.	Server management
4.	Security
5.	Web console
________________________________________
3. Integrated Developer Workflow
When developers build applications they need:
•	container image build
•	container image storage
•	CI/CD pipelines
Kubernetes does not provide these tools directly.
OpenShift provides them integrated.
Components
1. Container Registry
Stores container images.
Example:
Application → build image → store in registry → deploy
OpenShift includes a built-in container registry.
________________________________________
2. CI/CD Pipelines
Used for automated software delivery.
Pipeline steps:
Code → Build → Test → Deploy
OpenShift provides pipelines using Tekton.
________________________________________
3. Source-to-Image (S2I)
S2I converts source code directly into container images.
Example:
Developer pushes code to GitHub.
OpenShift automatically:
Source code → build container image → deploy
So developers do not need to manually create Dockerfiles.
________________________________________
4. Observability
Observability means monitoring and troubleshooting systems.
It includes:
•	monitoring
•	logging
•	metrics
OpenShift provides built-in tools.
Monitoring
Monitors:
•	cluster health
•	CPU usage
•	memory usage
•	pod status
Example command:
oc adm top nodes
Check resource usage.
________________________________________
Logging
Logs help diagnose problems.
Example logs:
•	application logs
•	container logs
•	cluster logs
Command example:
oc logs pod-name
________________________________________
5. Server Management
Kubernetes requires an operating system on nodes.
OpenShift uses:
RHEL CoreOS
RHEL CoreOS is the operating system used by OpenShift nodes.
Characteristics
•	immutable OS
•	optimized for containers
•	based on Red Hat Enterprise Linux
________________________________________
Immutable Operating System
Immutable means:
•	system files cannot be manually modified
•	updates happen automatically
Benefits:
•	increased security
•	stable system
•	consistent environment
________________________________________
Cluster Updates
OpenShift provides automated cluster updates.
For example:
•	OS updates
•	Kubernetes updates
•	platform updates
Command example:
oc get clusterversion
Shows cluster version.
________________________________________
6. Unified Management Tools
OpenShift provides multiple management tools.
1 Command Line Tool
CLI tool:
oc
Example commands:
Check pods:
oc get pods
Check nodes:
oc get nodes
Deploy application:
oc new-app nginx
________________________________________
2 Web Console
OpenShift provides a graphical web interface.
Through web console you can:
•	deploy applications
•	monitor pods
•	check logs
•	manage storage
•	manage networking
This is easier for beginners.
